<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerard of Csanád</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 980–<a href="/years/1046">1046</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Saint Gerard Sagredo, Saint Gellért</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerard of Csanád was a Venetian-born Benedictine monk who became tutor to Stephen I's son Emeric and, around 1030, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Csanád (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenad">Cenad</a>) in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> during the pagan uprising of 1046, according to legend thrown from the hill now named after him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. Canonised in 1083, he is venerated as a patron saint of Hungary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerard-of-csanad.jpg" alt="Gerard of Csanád" width="320" height="725" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Rocco_(Venice)_-_Statue_of_Saint_Gerard_of_Csan%C3%A1d.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Most information about Gerard survives in his legends, which contain many conventional elements of medieval saints' biographies. He was born in a noble Venetian family. Sources written centuries later associate it with the Sagredo or Morosini families. His parents, also named Gerard and Catherine, waited three years for his birth and baptised him George, since he was born on the feast of Saint George, 23 April. At the age of five he fell seriously ill, and his recovery was attributed to the prayers of the Benedictine monks of the San Giorgio Monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, to which his family sent him. He studied there and was later sent away to study grammar, music, philosophy and law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gerard left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on a pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> around 1020. Modern historians connect his departure with armed conflicts between the Orseolos and their opponents. A storm forced him to break his journey in a monastery near Istria, where Rasina persuaded him to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pecs">Pécs</a>, Bishop Maurus and Abbot Anastasius of Pécsvárad urged him to stay, and King Stephen I of Hungary, meeting him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a>, argued that preaching in Hungary would serve God best. Gerard agreed and became tutor to the king's heir, Emeric, though that role is mentioned only in his Long Life. He then lived for several years as a hermit in the Bakony Hills near Bakonybél, where he wrote homilies and commentaries that were later lost.</p><p>Around 1030 Stephen I made him the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the new Diocese of Csanád, formed after the royal army under Csanád defeated the chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahtum">Ajtony</a>, who had ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and Mureș region. The Annales Posonienses give 1030, though historians do not all trust the date. Twelve Benedictine monks accompanied him, seven of whom spoke Hungarian and interpreted for him. As a missionary bishop he baptised many converts. The Long Life says he built churches in every city of his diocese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gerard was killed on 24 September 1046 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vata-pagan-uprising">Vata pagan uprising</a>, with Bystrik and Buldus. Accounts differ. In one he was stoned, pierced with a lance and thrown from a cliff into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In another he was hauled in a cart to a hilltop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, now Gellért Hill, and rolled down it. Gerard was canonised in 1083, together with Stephen and Emeric. He is one of the patron saints of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and his feast day is 24 September.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gerard of Csanád” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stephen I was canonised in 1083, together with his son Emeric and Bishop <b>Gerard of Csanád</b>, and Ladislaus I in 1192; the dynasty became known for its saints, including Andrew II's daughter Elizabeth of Hungary and Béla IV's daughter Margaret.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1022" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gerard of Csanád"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1022">What was here in 1022? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
